  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 810 PM EST Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Drying out and becoming seasonably cool this \nweekend into early next week.\n\nLatest analysis reveals low pressure over coastal New England\nearly this evening, with the associated surface cold front now\npushing offshore of the mid-Atlantic coast. Aloft, a potent\nclosed low continues to slide east across the Eastern Great\nLakes into the interior northeast. This feature will push the \nsecondary/reinforcing cold frontal passage late tonight, which \nwill initiate some better mixing and more breezy conditions for \nlate tonight and Saturday. After some ground fog late this\nevening, cool, dry advection should scatter out any lingering\nfog and low clouds by sunrise Sat morning.\n\nMuch cooler and drier air is forecasted to advect into the \narea tomorrow, as chilly high pressure builds to the west\nthrough tomorrow. High temperatures settle back to near seasonal\naverages. High temperatures this weekend will be in the low to \nmid 60s, rising to the mid to upper 60s early next week, with \npotential for ~70F on Tuesday for a large portion of the area. \nTonight's lows will be moderate compared to the next coming days\nwith lows in the 40s W and N to mid 50s SE. Then, Sunday \nmorning's lows will be in the mid to upper 30s inland and low \n40s along the coast. Patchy frost is possible, particularly in \nthe piedmont and counties that have already ended their growing \nseason. Will continue to monitor trends for areas where the \ngrowing season has not yet ended. The recent ensemble guidance \nis continuing to show dry weather persisting at least into the \nmiddle of next week.",
